function solve(n, t) {
    // DP: dp[i][j][k][l] = number of sequences of length i ending with value j,
    // with k humps so far, and l indicating the last direction (0: down, 1: up)
    // Actually, we need to track the number of humps (peaks) and valleys.
    // Let's use: dp[i][lastVal][humps][valleys][lastDir]
    // But humps and valleys are related. For a camel with t humps, we need exactly t peaks and t-1 valleys.
    // Let's define:
    // dp[i][j][h][v][d] where:
    // i = position (1 to n)
    // j = y value at position i (1 to 4)
    // h = number of peaks so far
    // v = number of valleys so far
    // d = direction of the last segment (0: decreasing, 1: increasing)
    // 
    // A peak occurs at position i if i >= 2 and i <= n-1 and y[i-1] < y[i] > y[i+1]
    // A valley occurs at position i if i >= 2 and i <= n-1 and y[i-1] > y[i] < y[i+1]
    //
    // We can build the sequence step by step. When we add a new point, we can determine if the previous point was a peak or valley.
    // 
